                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/supergeometry+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %p This page collects material related to the book
          %ul
            %li#Grassmann44
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Grassmann"}> Hermann Grassmann
                ,
              %p
                %em Die Wissenschaft der extensiven Grössen oder die Ausdehnungslehre
              %p
                %em> Erster Teil, die lineale Ausdehnungslehre
                ,
              %p 1844
              %p
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.uni-potsdam.de/u/philosophie/grassmann/Werke/Hermann/Ausdehnungslehre_1844.pdf"}> pdf scan of original
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/dielinealeausde00grasgoog/page/n11"} Internet Archive copy
          %p
            which introduced for the first time basic concepts of what today is known as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} linear algebra
            (including
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+spaces"} affine spaces
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsors"} torsors
            over
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            %em exterior product
            (§37, §55) on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vectors"} vectors
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+algebra"} exterior algebra
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmann+algebra"} Grassmann algebra
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#SupercommutativeSuperalgebra"} below
          %p
            Grassmann advertizes his work (p. xxv) as being the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+quantity"} extensive quantity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+algebra"} exterior algebra
            he considered are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
          %p Prominent followups are:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giuseppe+Peano"}> Giuseppe Peano
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Calcolo Geometrico secondo l’Ausdehnungslehre di H. Grassmann preceduto dalle Operazioni della Logica Deduttiva
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://mathematica.sns.it/opere/138"} Mathematica Italiana opere 138
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://mathematica.sns.it/media/volumi/138/Calcolo%20geometrico%20secondo%20l'Ausdehnungslhere%20di%20H.%20Grassmann_bw.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            which introduces the modern concept of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Clifford"}> William Clifford
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Applications of Grassmann’s extensive algebra
              %strong 1
              4 (1878) 350-358 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/2369379"}> doi:10.2307/2369379
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/2369379"} jstor:2369379
          %p
            which introduces the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+algebra"} Clifford algebra
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#content"} Content
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#SupercommutativeSuperalgebra"} Supercommutative superalgebra
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Reception"} Reception
              %li
                %a{:href => "#discussion"} Discussion
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#content Content
          %h3#SupercommutativeSuperalgebra Supercommutative superalgebra
          %p#GrassmannDefinesSuperalgebra
            Here is Grassmann introducing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signs+in+supergeometry"} sign rule
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supercommutative+superalgebra"} supercommutative superalgebra
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/GrassmannGradedCommutativityI.jpg", :width => "460"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              from
              %a{:href => "#Grassmann44"} Grassmann 1844, p. 61
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/GrassmannGradedCommutativityII.jpg", :width => "460"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              from
              %a{:href => "#Grassmann44"} Grassmann 1844, p. 84
          %h2#Reception Reception
          %p The appreciation of Grassmann’s ideas took a long time:
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "https://plus.google.com/103061162497127117651/posts/f4HHK1XhUL1"} Drew Armstrong
          %blockquote
            %p
              Perhaps owing to the abstraction of his work, he was not recognized by the mathematical community and was more famous during his lifetime for his work in philology (including a translation of the Rig Veda). Despite his failure to achieve mathematical success, Grassmann never doubted that his work was significant. Here’s a striking quote from the preface of the 1862 second edition of the
              = succeed ":" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ausdehnungslehre"} Ausdehnungslehre
            %blockquote
              %p#CompletelyConvinced I remain completely confident that the labour I have expended on the science presented here and which has demanded a significant part of my life as well as the most strenuous application of my powers, will not be lost. It is true that I am aware that the form which I have given the science is imperfect and must be imperfect. But I know and feel obliged to state (though I run the risk of seeming arrogant) that even if this work should again remain unused for another seventeen years or even longer, without entering into the actual development of science, still that time will come when it will be brought forth from the dust of oblivion and when ideas now dormant will bring forth fruit. I know that if I also fail to gather around me (as I have until now desired in vain) a circle of scholars, whom I could fructify with these ideas, and whom I could stimulate to develop and enrich them further, yet there will come a time when these ideas, perhaps in a new form, will arise anew and will enter into a living communication with contemporary developments. For truth is eternal and divine.
            %p
              (Translation from Michael Crowe,
              = succeed ")" do
                %em A History of Vector Analysis
          %p#WhyNoOneHasTranslated
            Here is R. W. Genese in 1893 (
            %a{:href => "https://www.nature.com/articles/048517a0"}> Nature volume 48, page 517
            ) lamenting the delay in its translation:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Sir Robert Ball asks why no one has translated the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ausdehnungslehre"}> Ausdehnungslehre
              ” into English. The answer is as regretable as simple—it would not pay. The number of mathematicians who, after the severe courses of the universities, desire to extend their reading is very small. It is something that a respectable few seek to apply what they have already learnt. The first duty of those who direct the studies of the universities is to provide that students may leave in possession of all the best means of future investigation. That fifty years after publication the principles of the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ausdehnungslehre"}> Ausdehnungslehre
              ” should find no place in English mathematical education is indeed astonishing. Half the time given to such a wearisome subject as Lunar Theory would place a student in possession of many of the delightful surprises of Grassmann’s work, and set him thinking for himself. The “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ausdehnungslehre"}> Ausdehnungslehre
              ” has won the admiration of too many distinguished mathematicians to remain longer ignored. Clifford said of it: “I may, perhaps, be permitted to express my profound admiration of that extraordinary work, and my conviction that its principles will exercise a vast influence upon the future of mathematical science.” Useful or not, the work is “a thing of beauty,” and no mathematician of taste should pass it by. It is possible, nay, even likely, that its principles may be taught more simply; but the work should be preserved as a classic.
          %p Grassmann’s ideas were finally studied in
          %ul
            %li
              Hermann Hankel,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Vorlesungen über die Complexen Zahlen und ihre Functionen
          %p
            His ideas were developed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Kingdon+Clifford"} William Kingdon Clifford
            who turned Grassmann algebras into
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+algebras"} Clifford algebras
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Clifford"}> William Clifford
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Applications of Grassmann’s extensive algebra
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/2369379"} 10.2307/2369379
          %p
            Today
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmann+algebra"} Grassmann algebra
            is ubiquituous in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+algebra"}> de Rham algebra
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signs+in+supergeometry"} sign rule
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supercommutative+superalgebra"} supercommutative superalgebra
          %p
            This way Grassmann’s prediction on the eventual impact of his work did become true. From
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freeman+Dyson"} Freeman Dyson
            %em Missed opportunities
            (
            %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9904-1972-12971-9"}> doi:10.1090/S0002-9904-1972-12971-9
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p
              In the year 1844 two remarkable events occurred, the publication by Hamilton of his discovery of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternions"} quaternions
              = succeed ".\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ausdehnungslehre"} Ausdehnungslehre
          %p
            Grassmann also had a profound influence on the thought of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gottlob+Frege"} Gottlob Frege
          %ul
            %li
              Paolo Mancosu,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Abstraction and infinity
          %h2#discussion Discussion
          %p Discussion of the book includes
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere96
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Grassmann’s Dialectics and Category Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %em Hermann Günther Graßmann (1809–1877): Visionary Mathematician, Scientist and Neohumanist Scholar
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-015-8753-2_21"} 10.1007/978-94-015-8753-2_21
          %p and the similar text
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere95
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A new branch of mathematics, “The Ausdehnungslehre of 1844,” and other works. Open Court (1995), Translated by Lloyd C. Kannenberg, with foreword by Albert C. Lewis
              = succeed "," do
                %em Historia Mathematica Volume 32, Issue 1, February 2005, Pages 99–106
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.hm.2004.07.004"} 10.1016/j.hm.2004.07.004
          %p
            which says at one point that full appreciation of the
            %em Ausdehnungslehre
            requires concepts of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %blockquote
            %p
              The modern conceptual apparatus, involving levels of structure,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              preserving given
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful reduct functors
              between categories, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoints"} adjoints
              to such functors, etc., seems to be necessary for ordinary mortals to be able to find their way through the riches of Grassmann’s geometry.
          %p
            The first part of the introduction of the
            %em Ausdehnungslehre
            is concerned with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosophy
          %blockquote
            %p
              Grassmann insists that his reason for including it is an attempt to provide an orientation to help the student form for himself the proper estimation of the relation between general and particular at every stage of the learning process (
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere95"}> Lawvere 95
              ).
          %p
            The second part of the introduction, titled
            %em Survey of the general theory of forms
            considers key concepts of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity+law"} associativity law
            and states its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+law"} coherence law
            (§3).
          %p Grassmann writes that he uses the term “form” in place of “quantity” (German: “Grösse”) (Introduction A.3, §2). It is “forms” that his algebraic operations are defined on, and which are produced by these.
          %blockquote
            %p
              The last half of that introduction is essentially one of the first expositions of the rudimentary principles of what today might be called
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+algebra"} universal algebra
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Lawvere95"} Lawvere 95
          %p
            Curiously, while Grassmann complains (on p. xv) about the “unclarity and arbitrariness” of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegel"} Hegel
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/German+idealism"} German idealism
            %em Ausdehnungslehre
            has much the same sound as Hegel, notably it discusses “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+%28philosophy%29"}> categories
            ” such as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/being"} being
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/becoming"} becoming
            (p. xxii),
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+particular"} particulars
            (p.xx) and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dialectic"} dialectic
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unities+of+opposites"} opposites
            such as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} discrete
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊣
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} continuous
            (p.xxii) and, notably, of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensive+and+extensive+quantity"} intensive and extensive quantity
            (p. xxiv-xxv), which Grassmann advertizes as the very topic of his mathematical theory. That of course is the difference to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegel"} Hegel
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere95"} Lawvere 95
            Grassmannn’s formalization of the pair
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/being"} being
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/becoming"} becoming
            is via points and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vectors"} vectors
            in an
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objective+logic"} objective logic
            in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            (as discussed at
